   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to the provision of end\nstage renal disease services",
    "basePrintNo": "S149",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S149-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2801-a, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S149\n\nSPONSOR: GIANARIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to the provision of\nend stage renal disease services\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo clarify that diagnostic and treatment centers established exclusively\nto provide end stage renal disease services subject to Public Health Law\n§ 2801-a (15)(a) includes ambulatory surgery centers established exclu-\nsively to create and maintain vascular access necessary to dialyze\npatients with end stage renal disease.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends paragraph (a) of subdivision 15 of section 2801-a of\nthe public health law to add language that diagnostic and treatment\ncenters established exclusively to provide end stage renal disease\nservices includes ambulatory surgery centers established exclusively to\ncreate and maintain vascular access necessary to dialyze patients with\nend stage renal disease.\n\nSection two sets the effective date.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nClick here\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nParagraph (a) of subdivision 15 of section 2801-a of the public health\nlaw authorizes diagnostic and treatment centers established exclusively\nto provide end stage renal disease services to be operated by corpo-\nrations and limited liability companies whose stockholders and members\nare not natural persons. Inasmuch as life-sustaining hemodialysis treat-\nment necessarily depends upon the creation and maintenance of access to\nthe patient's blood stream, end stage renal disease services necessarily\nincludes the creation and maintenance of vascular access for end stage\nrenal disease patients.\n\nThis bill will make clear that a diagnostic and treatment center estab-\nlished exclusively to provide end stage renal disease services includes\nan ambulatory surgery center established exclusively to create and main-\ntain vascular access for end stage renal disease patients receiving\nhemodialysis.\n\nClarification that dialysis corporations are authorized to establish\nsingle-specialty ambulatory surgery centers established exclusively to\ncreate and maintain vascular access for hemodialysis patients will\nfacilitate the integration of dialysis treatment and dialysis vascular\naccess. A vascular access integration model has been proven to (1)\ndecrease catheter exposure time consistent with the Centers For Medicare\nand Medicaid Services (CMS) quality and cost containment goals, (2)\nincrease the use of arteriovenous fistulas (\"AVFs\") as the preferred\nmethod of vascular access for hemodialysis consistent with CMS's Fistula\nFirst Initiative, with a concomitant decrease in costs and vascular\ncomplication rates, (3) decrease mortality rates, (4) decrease the\nnumber of missed dialysis treatments due to access compromise, (5) lower\nhospital admission rates and inpatient days consistent with DSRIP goals,\nand (6) decrease overall costs per ESRD patient.\n\nMoreover, this Bill is consistent with CMS' ongoing efforts to reduce\nthe cost, and manage the care of, ESRD patients as reflected in its\nComprehensive ESRD Care (\"CEC\") Model and corresponding ESRD Seamless\nCare Organizations (\"ESCOs\") designed to coordinate all care for ESRD\nbeneficiaries.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2022: Passed Senate; Died in Health (Assembly)\n\n2021: Passed Senate; Died in Health (Assembly)\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nClick here\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
